.TP
\f[B]v2.29.1\f[R] released October 24th, 2024
Fix a segmentation fault that could occur during alignment in version
2.29.0, for example with \f[V]--uchime_ref\f[R].
Some improvements to code and documentation.
.TP
\f[B]v2.29.2\f[R] released December 20th, 2024
Fix a segmentation fault during clustering when the set of clusters is
empty.
Initial documentation in markdown format available on GitHub Pages.
.TP
\f[B]v2.29.3\f[R] released February 3rd, 2025
Released to mitigate a bug that occurs when compiling the
\f[V]align_simd.cc\f[R] file on x86_64 systems with the GNU C++ compiler
version 9 or later with the \f[V]-O3\f[R] optimization option.
It results in incorrect code that may cause bad alignments in some
circumstances.
We are investigating this issue further, but for now we recommend
compiling with the \f[V]-O2\f[R] flag.
The README.md file and the Dockerfiles have been updated to reflect
this.
The binaries released with this version will include this fix.
.TP
\f[B]v2.29.4\f[R] released February 14th, 2025
Adjust the window size used for chimera detection down from 64 to 32.
The window size was by accident increased from 32 to 64 in version
2.23.0, leading to somewhat fewer chimeras being predicted.
In addition, a compiler pragma has been included in align_simd.cc to
further protect the compiler from generating wrong code.
.TP
\f[B]v2.30.0\f[R] released February 27th, 2025
Add options \f[V]--n_mismatch\f[R], \f[V]--fastq_minqual\f[R], and
\f[V]--fastq_truncee_rate\f[R].
The \f[V]--n_mismatch\f[R] option will count N\[cq]s as mismatches in
alignments, which may be useful to get sensible alignments for sequences
with lots of N\[cq]s.
By default N\[cq]s are counted as matches.
Both the scoring and the counting of matches are affected.
The new \f[V]--fastq_minqual\f[R] option for the
\f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R] and \f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R] commands will
discard sequences with any bases with a quality score below the given
value.
The new \f[V]--fastq_truncee_rate\f[R] option for the same commands will
truncate sequences at the first position where the number of expected
errors per base is above the given value.
.TP
